  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Rain continues today, then drying out for early next \nweek.\n\nRain has begun moving into the region this early morning, though \nmost of it has been light rain and drizzle, as it is taking some \ntime to erode the dry air that has been in place over the region for \nseveral days. Should see rain expanding in coverage over the area \nafter sunrise, with the highest probabilities for rain and \nhighest rainfall amounts along and east of the Blue Ridge \nFoothills. Still some model spread in rainfall amounts, but \nexpected 24 hour totals are between 0.40\" to 0.50\" in the \nPiedmont, and up to 0.10\" along the I81 corridor, with a sharp \nwestern edge to the amounts, only a few hundredths of an inch \nwest of the New River and Roanoke Valley. These values have \ndecreased in recent forecast cycles, but there is still a 20% \nchance per the NBM of 24 hour totals through Monday morning \nexceeding an inch in the VA Southside. Overall, these values are\nnot expected to lead to any flooding concerns, but there may be\nsome overflows of ditches and drainage areas due to recent leaf\ndebris clogging these areas, but any rain in this region would \nbe beneficial, given the persistent Severe to Extreme drought \nconditions across southern VA and eastern NC. \n\nStrong high pressure will build across the northeast US after \ntoday's system, with dry and brisk conditions ensuing for the early \npart of the work week. High temperatures will drop from the 50s and \n60s today, to 40s and 50s by Tuesday, with breezy northwesterly \nwinds. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Confidence continues to increase in precipitation for \nThanksgiving.\n\nBy the middle of the work week, model consensus has a 500mb\ntrough digging into the Mississippi Valley region, while a \nsurface low develops along a frontal boundary across the \nsoutheast US, deepening as it tracks northward along/near the \nEast Coast. This eventually phases with the upper trough, but \nmodel guidance diverges significantly with this evolution. \nNonetheless, confidence is increasing that this system  will \nbring ample moisture and widespread precipitation into the \nregion around Thanksgiving. Cold air damming already in place \nwith the surface high wedging down against the mountains will \nsupport the potential for some wintry precipitation over the \nmountains. However, recent guidance has trended slightly \nwarmer, with more warm air aloft, thus leading to more rain than\nsnow. Current thinking is that any wintry precip will be \nconfined to the highest elevations. That being said, \nconsiderable uncertainty remains with this system so the \nforecast may change in subsequent days, but given the proximity \nof this system to major travel days around Thanksgiving, we \nencourage the public to stay up to date with the forecast. \n\nThe system moves into the northeast by Friday or so, with high \npressure building over the southeast and into the local area, \nbringing a return to dry weather for the end of the week.",
